speechtext:
The hon. member will have heard me when I have finished; I propose to give very good reasons for the course of action I propose to take.
As I have said on former occasions, I do not think the present system is the best possible 78594-100}
system we could have. However, it is the system in existence at the present time, and I intend to act in harmony with it. I have said that the senate committee deal with these questions, within the limits which are imposed upon them, in manner which commands the approval of those who have seen them taking evidence. I know that they are handicapped; I know that they cannot go as far in the way of granting redress to injured parties as we would like to have them go, and as we believe a properly established court could go, but, at the same time, I think it is a mistake to give the public the impression that these questions are not being dealt with seriously by a body of men competent to deal with them. Whether or not I read the evidence taken in connection with these cases, I have confidence in the senate committee before whom that evidence is adduced, and when they submit these questions to us I believe we are justified in assuming that they have reached the proper conclusion. I shall not vote against these bills because I have not read the evidence, but will assume that the senate committee has properly discharged its duty; that these bills come to us after being properly considered, and, therefore, should be endorsed by the house.
